<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amasra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amastris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amasra is a small port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, in Bartın Province. The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amastris-queen">Amastris</a>, it was a Byzantine port before Genoese merchants made it one of their trading stations in the 13th and 14th centuries, building walls whose remains survive. The Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> took it in 1460.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amasra.jpg" alt="Amasra" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jorge Franganillo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amasra_(9310207038).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Amasra lay in the ancient region of Paphlagonia, where the original city seems to have been called Sesamus. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amastris-queen">Amastris</a> comes from Amastris, niece of the last Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a>, wife of Dionysius, tyrant of Heraclea, and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>. After separating from Lysimachus she combined four small Ionian colonies, Sesamus, Cytorus, Cromna and Tium, into a new community on a peninsula. Tium soon detached itself, and Sesamus served as the acropolis.</p><p>Its tyrant Eumenes presented the city to Ariobarzanes of Pontus in about 265–260 BCE, and it stayed in the Pontic kingdom until Lucius Lucullus captured it in 70 BCE in the second Mithridatic War. The younger Pliny, as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> and Pontus, described it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> as a handsome city and obtained permission to cover an open drain. A coin of Trajan's time gives it the title Metropolis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Byzantine era the acropolis became a fortress and a church was built that still survives. The Rus sacked the city during the First Russo-Byzantine War in the 830s. Historian Speros Vryonis states that in the 9th century local industry, trade and farming made it one of the more prosperous towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and the writer Niketas David Paphlagon called it the Eye of Paphlagonia.</p><p>Seljuk raids in the 12th century reduced it to a small town. In the 13th century it changed hands several times: a Georgian army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-komnenos">David Komnenos</a> seized it, making it a possession of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in 1204, and it was later captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>. In 1261 it passed to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. Genoese rule ended when the Ottoman Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> conquered the Anatolian shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Amasra is the seat of Amasra District in Bartın Province and had 6,098 inhabitants in 2021. It has two islands, Büyük ada and Tavşan adası. Amasra Castle was begun in the Roman period, its walls built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, and its front walls and gates by the Genoese in the 14th and 15th centuries. The Fatih Mosque was a Byzantine church of the 9th century, converted after 1460.</p><p>The archaeological museum holds a statue of the snake god Glykon, described as a fraudulent creation of a local entrepreneur in Roman imperial times. Early in its Christian history the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amastris-queen">Amastris</a> had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palmas</a>, mentioned in a letter of Dionysius of Corinth, and it is now a Catholic titular see. Amasra belongs to the European Association of Historic Towns and Regions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amasra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Treaty of Nymphaeum of 1261 opened the Black Sea to Genoa, Genoese merchants established themselves at <b>Amasra</b> (Samastri), an old Byzantine port on a pair of headlands, which became one of their stations on the coast between Constantinople and Caffa.</blockquote><a href="/state/genoese-amasra">Genoese Amasra</a></li></ul></section>
